      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Tying knots in light ﬁelds\nHridesh Kedia,1, ∗Iwo Bialynicki-Birula,2 Daniel Peralta-Salas,3 and William T.M. Irvine1\n1University of Chicago, Physics department and the James Franck institute, 929 E 57th st. Chicago, IL, 60605\n2Center for Theoretical Physics, Polish Academy of Sciences Al. Lotnikow 32/46, 02-668 Warsaw, Poland\n3Instituto de Ciencias Matem´aticas, Consejo Superior de Investigaciones Cient´ıﬁcas, 28049 Madrid, Spain\nWe construct a new family of null solutions to Maxwell’s equations in free space whose ﬁeld lines encode all\ntorus knots and links. The evolution of these null ﬁelds,",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "not-summarised"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/arxiv-1302.0342v1/blocks/prose-page-002",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Page 2",
      "pageStart": 2,
      "pageEnd": 2,
      "target": "../sections/page-002.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "with it, guaranteeing their topological preservation. In this\nletter we show how constructions based on Bateman’s method\n[18] or equivalently a spinor formalism can lead to novel null\nsolutions that encode knots of diﬀerent kinds, and evolve in\na smooth manner like the Hopﬁon solution. The combination\nof a null electromagnetic ﬁeld formalism with a topological\nconstruction, leading to a family of knotted null solutions is\nthe central result of this paper.\nThe Hopﬁon solution – The Hopﬁon electromagnetic ﬁeld\n(Fig. 1) is a light-beam-like propagating solution, with the\ncurious property that all",
      "truncated": true,
